The secondary \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption site in Cu-BTT (Fig. 1) is rotationally disordered above the \([\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}]^{7-}\) cluster. The closest oxygen atom of carbon dioxide bound at this site is approximately \(3.27\mathrm{~\AA~}\) from the \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) and \(3.44\mathrm{~\AA~}\) from the center of the tetrazolate ring, distances that are in agreement with what is expected based on the van der Waals radii of oxygen and chlorine. Interestingly, the extra-framework cations in Fe-BTT also reside just above the \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) anion in the \([\mathbf{M}_{4}\mathbf{Cl}]^{7-}\) cluster and partially block the secondary adsorption site in this structure. In addition to the blocking of a large number of primary adsorption sites due to solvent molecules, the positioning of this cation may also influence the significant drop observed in the \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) for Fe-BTT relative to \(\mathrm{Cr}-\) and Cu-BTT. As was the case for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) bound at site I, the DFT calculated binding geometries for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at site II match well with the experimentally-determined values (Table 2). The largest deviation was observed for CrBTT, for which the DFT calculations revealed a \(\mathrm{Cl}^{\ldots}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})\) distance that is \({\sim}0.1\mathrm{~\AA~}\) longer than that observed experimentally. The experimentally and theoretically determined structures for Cu-BTT are overlaid in Fig. 4 (see also Fig. \(\mathrm{S}20\dagger\) ).
At site III, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is rotationally disordered between two carbon atoms of the \(\mathrm{BTT}^{3-}\) linker and located at a \(\mathrm{C}^{\ldots}\mathrm{O}\) distance of \({\sim}3.27\mathrm{~\AA~}\) from the framework wall. Although this site is twice as abundant as site II, the adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at site III is expected to be weaker given its lower occupation observed via in situ neutron diffraction. It should be noted that while the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules located at sites III and IV are somewhat disordered, the intermolecular \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) distances for these two sites are shorter than those of the closest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) -framework distances. It may be that the population of site II helps to stabilize the population of these two additional adsorption sites, an observation that is supported by a pronounced plateau in \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) at higher loadings (Fig. 2). It is also possible that \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules located at sites III
and IV afford additional stabilization to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules located at site II (Fig. 1), in a manner similar to that suggested in an earlier study on \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) binding in the metal–organic framework \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\).60
Positional disorder in the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules bound at sites III and IV precluded periodic DFT analysis, and therefore we turned to force fields to quantify any stabilizing influence of neighboring \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules on the adsorbed gas at these positions. For this analysis, we arranged the site III and IV \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules at their experimental unit cell positions around site II \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with the DFT-determined binding geometry. We found the strength of the interaction between site II and site III \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules to be \(-4.58\), \(-3.13\), and \(-3.26\;\mathrm{kJ\;mol}^{-1}\) for Cu-, Cr-, and Fe-BTT, respectively. Interactions between \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) located at site II and site IV were found to be smaller, although of the same order of magnitude. Considering the magnitude of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) binding enthalpies at site II (Table 2), it is expected that interactions with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules at sites III and IV are unlikely to have a large impact on the site II binding enthalpy. However, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules at sites III and IV exhibit weaker interactions with the framework compared to that of site II and may be primarily stabilized by interactions with the closest site II molecule.
Because the strength of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) binding at site I is dependent on the identity of the framework metal and the binding strength at site II is not, the theoretical difference between the site I and site II binding energies follows the same trend across the M-BTT series as the site I binding energy \(\mathrm{(Fe>Cr>Cr)}\) . DFT calculations show that \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) binds more strongly at site I than site II in Fe-, Cr-, and Cu-BTT by 27.2, \({\sim}10\) , and \(2.6\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1}\) , respectively. The higher binding strength at site I is supported by the higher experimentally observed site I occupancy at low \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) loadings in diffraction experiments. We note that while an energy difference as small as \({\sim}3\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1}\) is enough to distinguish sites I and II and lead to sequential occupation of adsorption sites at \(10\;\mathrm{K}\) , at room temperature there is enough thermal energy such that there is no strongly preferred adsorption site. A similar phenomenon has been observed for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) binding in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\) using powder neutron diffraction, wherein thermodynamic preference for a given binding site at \(150\,\mathrm{~K~}\) is lost at higher temperatures.60 Room-temperature \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms for Cr-, Fe- and Cu-BTT plotted on semi-log and logarithmic axes are shown in Fig. S18 and \(\mathrm{S}19.1^{\circ}\) On a semi-log scale, Crand Cu-BTT exhibit Langmuir-type \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption behavior, while in Fe-BTT \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) appears to bind most strongly at low pressures. This latter observation is supported by DFT calculations, which predict the largest difference in site I and II binding energies for Fe-BTT. The combined theoretical and experimental work presented here suggests that differences in binding energy of \({>}20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1}\) may be needed to observe a truly sequential loading of different binding sites in room temperature adsorption isotherms.
## Comparison with previous computational work
It is of interest to compare the DFT results obtained here to previous computational work by Poloni et al., which examined
\(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption at the primary site in M-BTT for both existing and hypothetical analogs.35 The results in the previous work35 were obtained using atomic orbital basis sets and a \(\mathrm{PBE}+\mathrm{D}2+\) U van der Waals corrected functional, whereas this work used a plane wave basis set and the rev-vdW-DF2 \(^+\) U functional. Additionally, the two studies differ in the placement of the extra-framework cations. Both of these factors will contribute to differences in the geometric parameters and binding energies reported in the two studies; however, overall trends can be examined.
In particular, the site I binding energies and trend in M\(\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})\) distances reported previously are in good agreement both with those in this work and with the experimental isosteric heats of adsorption. This supports the conclusion that the interaction with the \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\) cation dominates and this site is not particularly sensitive to the position of the framework cation. On the other hand, the site I binding geometries from this study more closely match the diffraction results. For example, the M\(\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})\) binding distances at the primary adsorption sites were on average \(0.045\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) closer to the experimental values compared to those reported previously. Our distances are slightly shorter than experiment while those of Poloni et al. are slightly longer. This could be due to the fact that the rev-vdW-DF2 functional was reparameterized specifically to improve bond distances while maintaining good binding energies; however, functional dependence in this system has not been systematically examined. The previous study also observed that stronger \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) binding correlated with deviations from the expected linear geometry of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and shorter distances between the tetrazole nitrogen and the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) carbon. In the diffraction results presented here, neither of these trends is observed. Although our DFT calculations started from the experimental structure, we observe the same trend in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) bending as Poloni et al. Moreover, the distance between the tetrazole nitrogen and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) carbon shows no clear dependence on the binding energy and the trend between the metals is different in the experiment \(\mathrm{Cr}>\mathrm{Cu}>\mathrm{Fe})\) , our calculations \(\mathrm{(Cu>Fe>Cr)}\) , and those of Poloni et al. \(\mathrm{(Cu>Cr>Fe)}\) . However, both experiment and Poloni et al. report the shortest \(\mathbf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) distance for the strongest binding metal. Additionally, in our calculations for Fe-BTT sodium migrated closer to Cl than the experimentally observed Fe cation. It is possible that the location and identity of the extra framework Fe cations has an impact on the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) binding geometry.
The diffraction data presented in this work has created the opportunity for a future benchmarking study to determine the influence of different DFT functionals on the accurate prediction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption properties in the M-BTT framework series. Relative differences between \(\mathrm{Cr-}\) and Fe-BTT structures computed in this work compared to the previous study may have to do with the placement of sodium cations, the respective choices of functional, and the choices in the level-of-theory. Furthermore, the specific identity of the extra-framework cations may play an important role in counterbalancing intraframework forces. For example, DFT studies exploring whether extra-framework cations with different ionic radii and partial charge could occupy different sites based on steric
effects, which has been suggested in the literature,61 have not been performed.
## Conclusions
We have demonstrated that in situ neutron diffraction can provide insights into how to optimize existing metal–organic frameworks for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption (e.g., via metal substitution) while also providing detailed structural information that can be used to validate computational methods aimed at predicting the behavior of metal–organic frameworks with open metal sites. Indeed, in this study, calculated \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) binding energies and geometries were in good agreement with the experimental results, and such validation of computational approaches for adsorption applications opens the door to predicting the behavior of synthesized and even hypothetical metal–organic frameworks.
The foregoing results also provide experimental evidence that the strength of adsorption at the open metal site in the M-BTT series can be rationalized based on metal identity, including the partial charge on the electropositive \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\) cations. This observation is confirmed by both prior computational work and the results of DFT calculations presented here.35 DFT calculations also suggest that the heat of adsorption at site II is not dependent on metal identity. Strong \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorbents, like Fe-BTT, display sequential adsorption behaviour, which could potentially be exploited for applications such as \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion. This study shows the need for combined experimental and computational studies capable of predicting framework properties, a process that might eventually allow the rapid identification of target materials for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture, as well as a host of other applications.
